WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.437
This slide combines two ideas: normalize messy text first, then apply fallback logic.

00:00:06.437 --> 00:00:11.197
Suppose our contact priority is email first, then mobile phone, then home phone,

00:00:11.197 --> 00:00:12.482
and finally No contact provided.

00:00:12.482 --> 00:00:20.012
If we feed raw fields directly into COALESCE, an email containing only spaces may be treated as available

00:00:20.012 --> 00:00:22.067
because it is technically not NULL.

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:28.787
That would block the fallback even though the email is unusable.

00:00:28.787 --> 00:00:32.107
So each contact field is cleaned first.

00:00:32.107 --> 00:00:35.952
We trim the text. Then NULLIF converts an empty cleaned value to NULL.

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:37.512
Only after that do we use COALESCE.

00:00:37.512 --> 00:00:44.867
This creates a very natural pipeline: standardize what “missing” means, then choose the first usable value.

00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:50.232
Run the query and inspect the edge cases.

00:00:50.232 --> 00:00:54.492
Some patients should fall back from email to mobile or home phone.

00:00:54.492 --> 00:00:59.707
A patient with no usable contact values should receive the final display label.

00:00:59.707 --> 00:01:02.777
You can use the same pattern in many projects.

00:01:02.777 --> 00:01:07.437
A shipping system may choose primary address, then secondary address.

00:01:07.437 --> 00:01:12.042
A customer profile may prefer work email, then personal email.

00:01:12.042 --> 00:01:18.292
A product catalog may prefer official description, then short description, then a placeholder.

00:01:18.292 --> 00:01:22.197
The order inside COALESCE is not merely technical.

00:01:22.197 --> 00:01:27.241
It represents business priority. That priority should be chosen intentionally.
